Roza Farabaugh is an artist who's  currently living and working in berlin  she has been seen quite widely in Europe  she's been in two Venice Biennale her  work has been in major museums however  she's had very little exposure in the  United States so I knew that she was one  of the first people I wanted to present  here so in this exhibition here at this  Center I'm showing for the first time in  the United States a larger overview of  my work so film installation but  sculptural works as well plus a new  piece the color out of space that is now  showing you for the first time as a  sculptural work Rosa initially studied  film as well as fine arts and that's  something that's very clear in this  exhibition and throughout her work which  really uses  and celluloid film as theme and material  what you see in the gallery are a number  of projector sculptures that make the  mechanism of projection very obvious I'm  very interested in working with a film  in a sculptural way so and creating  objects out of off the film and also  working very spatially and site-specific  with the material and and images we  early on decided that we would structure  the exhibition around ideas of time and  landscape roses very interested in sort  of deep time geological time time spans  that are sort of not grasped the ball by  the human mind and the way in which that  becomes evident in landscapes in general  I'm interested in traces that society  leaves around us so I'm trying to to  make a sense of those traces and how we  perceive them and how time plays a role  in that and it's it's a kind of  archeological approach to toot-toot I'm  Rosa engages with some of the same  topics or questions as many of the  scientists at MIT do for example the new  piece and the show called the color out  of space uses images of planets and  stars gathered at an observatory and she  has created a soundtrack in which  astronomers and writers and artists sort  of reflect on the universe both  scientifically and poetically and you  kind of come to understand that in some  ways the ways of thinking about this  material are not so different and then  you know some of the works are simply  quite beautiful  you
 

